UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 19, Lesson 2: Conflict role plays', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Conflict role plays let us practise calm words and fair solutions in made-up situations. Players stay in role for a short time, use a respectful tone and then step out to talk about what worked. Stay safe by using pretend situations, no touching and an agreed ''stop'' signal. Swapping roles helps us understand the other person''s feelings.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIn a role play someone says, ''You always ruin games!'' Rewrite it as an ''I'' statement and add a request.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks for something clear.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite and perform three conflict role plays with a family member (sharing equipment, a rule disagreement and a misunderstanding). Use an ''I'' statement and a calm tone in each, agree a stop signal, swap roles and write what you would change.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Conflict role plays let us practise calm words and fair solutions in made-up situations. Players stay in role for a short time, use a respectful tone and then step out to talk about what worked. Stay safe by using pretend situations, no touching and an agreed ''stop'' signal. Swapping roles helps us understand the other person''s feelings.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write and perform three conflict role plays with a family member (sharing equipment, a rule disagreement and a misunderstanding). Use an ''I'' statement and a calm tone in each, agree a stop signal, swap roles and write wh Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks for something clear. Reasoning: I used conflict role play by attending to a stop signal rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life. Correction: Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks… Why: the correction restores accurate use of conflict role play.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating conflict role play on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with conflict role play, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks… This matches the lesson skill for conflict role play.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks…","Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"In a role play someone says, ''You always ruin games!'' Rewrite it as an ''I'' statement and add a request. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Conflict role plays let us practise calm words and fair solutions in made-up situations. Players stay in role for a short time, use a respectful tone and then…","Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks…","Careful use of a stop signal with clear evidence.","Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising conflict role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a stop signal, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a stop signal used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising conflict role play. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks…","Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about conflict role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about conflict role play."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a stop signal?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example: A says, ''You''ve had the ball for ages and I feel left out. Can we take turns?'' B says, ''Okay, I''ll pass it to you after five…","Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Conflict role plays let us practise calm words and fair solutions in made-up situations. Players stay in… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Write and perform three conflict role plays with a family member (sharing equipment, a rule disagreement and a misunderstanding). Use an ''I''…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of conflict role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating conflict role play on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks for something clear. Reasoning: I used conflict role play by attending to a stop signal rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with conflict role play, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life. Correction: Rewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks… Why: the correction restores accurate use of conflict role play.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w19-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise conflict role play.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use conflict role play with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a stop signal and explain why it matters.","I can practise swapping roles independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a calm tone needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Conflict role plays let us practise calm words and fair solutions in made-up situations. Players stay in role for a short time, use a respectful tone and then step out to talk about what worked. Stay safe by using pretend situations, no touching and an agreed ''stop'' signal.…","options":["Conflict role plays let us practise calm words and fair solutions in made-up situations. Players stay in role for a short time, use a respectful tone and then step out to talk about what worked. Stay safe by using pretend situations, no touching and an agreed ''stop'' signal. Swapping roles helps us understand the other person''s feelings.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about conflict role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: A says, ''You''ve had the ball for ages and I feel left out. Can we take turns?'' B says, ''Okay, I''ll pass it to you after five throws.'' They step out and talk: the ''I'' statement and calm tone worked, and the number made the offer clear. Stop signal: a raised hand.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIn a role play someone says, ''You always ruin games!'' Rewrite it as an ''I'' statement and add a request.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks for something clear.","explain":"Conflict role plays let us practise calm words and fair solutions in made-up situations. Players stay in role for a short time, use a respectful tone and then step out to talk about what worked. Stay safe by using pretend situations, no touching and an agreed ''stop'' signal. Swapping roles helps us understand the other person''s feelings.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Blame and an I statement.","caption":"Name the feeling.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Blame</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">you always ruin games</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">I statement</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">I felt upset when</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: A says, ''You''ve had the ball for ages and I feel left out. Can we take turns?'' B says, ''Okay, I''ll pass it to y Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: A says, ''You''ve had the ball for ages and I feel left out. Can we take turns?'' B says, ''Okay, I''ll pass it to y Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about conflict role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: A says, ''You''ve had the ball for ages and I feel left out. Can we take turns?'' B says, ''Okay, I''ll pass it to you after five throws.'' They step out and talk: the ''I'' statement and calm tone worked, and the number made the offer clear. Stop signal: a raised hand. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Role play safety.","caption":"Agree a stop signal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stop signal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">raised hand</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Swap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">play the other role</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about conflict role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life. Here is a more helpful example: Example: A says, ''You''ve had the ball for ages and I feel left out. Can we take turns?'' B says, ''Okay, I''ll pass it to you after five throws.'' They step out and talk: the ''I'' statement and calm tone worked, and the number made the offer clear. Stop signal: a raised hand.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''I felt upset when the game stopped and I would like us to start again.'' It names the feeling and the event, has no ''always'' or put-down, and asks for something clear.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking conflict role plays should be loud or rude to look real. Practise respectful words so they become easy to use in real life. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about conflict role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nConflict role plays let us practise calm words and fair solutions in made-up situations. Players stay in role for a short time, use a respectful tone and then step out to talk about what worked. Stay safe by using pretend situations, no touching and an agreed ''stop'' signal. Swapping roles helps us understand the other person''s feelings. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 19, Lesson 2: Conflict role plays","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Friendships and respect","week":19,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: A says, ''You''ve had the ball for ages and I feel left out. Can we take turns?'' B says, ''Okay, I''ll pass it to you after five throws.'' They step out and talk: the ''I'' statement and calm tone worked, and the number made the offer clear. Stop signal: a raised hand.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Blame and an I statement.","caption":"Name the feeling.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Blame</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">you always ruin games</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">I statement</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">I felt upset when</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Role play safety.","caption":"Agree a stop signal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stop signal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">raised hand</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Swap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">play the other role</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w19-l2' AND fam